 ],
 "literal": "May perish my mind! Intense is her courage indeed! / To be women of the ancient house is fitting indeed! / For the battle that occurred on a former day, her elder brother, / having struck an elephant, perished in the battlefield. / For the battle that occurred yesterday, her husband, / having turned back a great herd of cattle, died there. / Today also, hearing the battle drum, with eagerness and excitement, / giving a spear into his hand, spreading and draping a white garment, / smearing oil on his sparse-haired tuft, / she who has no other than this one son, / sends him, saying, \"Go, looking towards the battlefront!\"",
 "translation": "May my mind perish! How fierce is her resolve! It is truly fitting that she is a woman of an ancient, noble house. In the battle of a former day, her elder brother struck down an elephant and died on the field. In yesterday's battle, her husband fell there while turning back a great cattle raid. And today, hearing the battle drum, filled with eager excitement, she gives a spear into her only son's hand, drapes him in a fresh white cloth, smears oil on his sparse-haired tuft, and sends him off, saying, 'Go, face the battlefront!'",
 "modern": "என் நெஞ்சம் அழியட்டும்! இவளுடைய துணிவு மிகவும் கொடியது (வியக்கத்தக்கது). பழமையான வீரக்குடியின் பெண் என்று சொல்லப்படுவது இவளுக்கு மிகவும் பொருந்தும். முந்தைய நாள் நடந்த போரில், இவளுடைய அண்ணன் யானையைக் கொன்று போர்க்களத்திலேயே மடிந்தான். நேற்று நடந்த போரில், இவளுடைய கணவன் பகைவரின் ஆநிரையைத் தடுத்து அங்கேயே மடிந்தான். இன்றும் போர்ப்பறையின் ஒலியைக் கேட்டு, மகிழ்ச்சியோடு மயங்கி, தன் ஒரே மகனுக்குக் கையில் வேலைக் கொடுத்து, அவனுக்கு வெண்ணிற ஆடையை உடுத்தி, அவனது குடுமி முடியில் எண்ணெயைத் தடவி, \"போர்க்களத்தை நோக்கிச் செல்!\" என்று கூறி அனுப்புகிறாள்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Okkūr Mācāttiyār speaks in awe of the fierce courage of a mother belonging to an ancient warrior clan. The poem describes a household that has suffered devastating losses in consecutive days of war: the woman's brother died fighting an elephant on the first day, and her husband died defending cattle on the second. On the third day, hearing the battle drums, she does not hesitate or grieve. Instead, she lovingly yet resolutely prepares her only remaining young son, smearing oil on his youthful tuft of hair, dressing him in white, handing him a spear, and sending him to the front lines. This poem belongs to the Vākai tiṇai and the Mūtiṉ Mullai tuṟai, which celebrates the ancestral bravery and sacrifice of noble warrior families.",
 "model": "gemini-3.5-flash",
 "generated": "2026-11-26"
}